[38;5;246m⏺ [39mUser answered Claude's questions:
[38;5;246m  ⎿  · Where should task data live? → localStorage, no abstraction (Recommended)[39m

[38;5;231m⏺[39m Locked: [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m, browser-only, no storage abstraction.

  Now, what a task [3mis[0m. This is a stored format, so it's the other decision that's annoying to change later — existing
  [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m records need migrating once real data exists.

  One thing worth surfacing now, because it changes this answer: in a single-user app, the only notification that
  genuinely earns its keep is [1m"this task is due soon."[0m It's the one change that happens while you're not looking,
  without needing other people. If you want notifications to be worth anything later, a due date isn't optional — it's
  the feature that makes them meaningful. Everything else (priority, tags, assignee, notes) is independent of
  notifications and can be added anytime without migration pain.

  So my recommendation is a lean record with the due date in from day one:

  { id, title, done, dueDate?, createdAt, updatedAt }

  — and deliberately [3mnot[0m priority, tags, subtasks, notes, or projects. Each of those is cheap to add later and none of
  them are needed to find out whether you'll use this thing.
